s/- Digit substitutions by a non-affine permutation of `F_p` are finite-state\n  maps of this wild kind\./- Digit substitutions by a non-affine permutation of `F_p` are finite-state\n  maps of this wild kind. Non-affine is sufficient, not necessary./;
s/  on a dense open set\. Then item 1 says the pieces of the generators generate a\n  subgroup of `PGL_2\(Q_p\)` that is not finitely generated\. So some generator has\n  infinitely many pieces, involving infinitely many primes\./  on a dense open set. By item 1, the pieces of the generators generate a\n  subgroup `Δ` of `PGL_2(Q_p)` containing every `d_q`, `q != p`. So `Δ` is not\n  finitely generated, by the following lemma, and some generator has infinitely\n  many pieces.\n  - **Lemma.** A finitely generated `Δ <= PGL_2(Q_p)` contains `d_q` for only\n    finitely many primes `q`.\n  - *Proof.* Lift the generators to `GL_2(Q_p)`, and let `S` be the ring\n    generated by their entries and the inverses of their determinants. If `d_q`\n    lies in `Δ`, some `λ diag(q,1)` lies in `GL_2(S)`. Then `λ, λq in S` and\n    `λ^2 q in S^x`, so `q^-1 = λ^2 (λ^2 q)^-1 in S`.\n  - By the Nullstellensatz, `S` maps into `O_K[1\/M]` for some number field `K`\n    and some `M` independent of `q`. There `q` is a unit, so `q | M`, by taking\n    norms./;
